  "transcript": "Amazon is kicking AI shopping agents out of its store. Walmart just built them a private agents with a spending limit. On October 21st, Walmart announced the agent gateway. AI agents can search Walmart and Sam's Club and check out for you. Walmart says it already works with ChatGPT, Gemini, and Meta's Muse. Next month, every bot gets its own profile inside your account. Right now, an AI agent shops like a very fast intern. It opens the website, reads the page, and clicks buttons built for humans. That's slow and it breaks every time someone moves a button. The gateway skips the website, agents plug in over MCP, a standard connector for AI tools, and get product price and stock straight from Walmart. Your profile says the rules, approve every purchase, the default, or let the bot spend up to a limit. 50 bucks of paper towels, sure. 200 bucks of TV, maybe not. Now, Amazon. In September, it blocked Meta's Muse for never identifying itself or asking permission. Here's the part nobody says out loud. An agent never sees an ad, nor sponsor products, nor impulse add to cart, nor private offer sale. For Amazon, every agent order is an ad nobody saw. Walmart is betting the other way, lose the ad view, win the order. The catch, the gateway is an invite only better and Walmart picks who gets in, open door with a bouncer. Here's the test I'd run before trusting any of this. Ask three agents to reorder the same paper towels, log where each one buys, what it pays, and whether it keeps your usual brand. If it switches brands, your loyalty program just lost to a spreadsheet. Amazon can lock the door, but your customers won't which is shop where they're welcome. To treat your product feed like a storefront, the next shopper might not have eyes.",
